Answer: A Java program contains a class and a main() method. Program execution begins from the main() method. The basic program used in the example is:

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

Output: Hello World The System.out.println() statement is used to display output on the console.

Answer: Both print() and println() are used to display output. print() keeps the cursor on the same line after printing, whereas println() moves the cursor to the next line. Example:

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.print("Hello ");
System.out.print("World");
}
}
```

Output: Hello World Here, both values are printed on the same line. Using println():

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("Hello");
System.out.println("World");
}
}
```

Output: Hello World Therefore: print() → prints and keeps the cursor on the same line println() → prints and moves the cursor to the next line

Answer: The value of one variable can be used in an expression, and the result can be stored in another variable. The example uses:

```java
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
int A = 10;
int C = A + 10;
System.out.println("Some value");
System.out.println(C);
}
}
```

Output: Some value 20 Here: A = 10 C = A + 10 C = 20 The expression A + 10 processes the value stored in A, and the resulting value 20 is stored in C. The + operator also has another use when one operand is a string. For example:

```java
System.out.println("Some value " + C);
```

Output: Some value 20 Here, + joins the string and the value of C.

Answer: When an integer is larger than the maximum range of long, the BigInteger class can be used. The class belongs to: java.math.BigInteger A BigInteger is a non-primitive type and is used for integers that cannot be represented by the primitive integer types. The basic example is:

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
BigInteger A = new BigInteger("9223372036854775808");
System.out.println(A);
}
}
```

This allows integers larger than the long range to be represented without being restricted to the fixed 64-bit long range.
